1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
When you give Claude a set of tools, you hit a problem right away.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:10,000
It has to choose which ones to call, and it makes that choice by reading the words that you wrote.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Get the description wrong, and the right tool sits unused.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,000
This lesson covers writing tool definitions that Claude will actually route to.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Every tool you define has three parts, and it helps to name them up front.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
First is the name. That's the unique identifier your code and the API use to invoke the tool,

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
and it follows a strict pattern of letters, digits, underscores, and hyphens.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Second is the description. This is plain text prose that says what the tool does, when to use it,

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:41,000
and how it behaves.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
It's the part Claude reads to decide whether to call the tool at all.

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Third is the input schema, a JSON schema object that declares the parameters Claude may pass and their types.

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Name to invoke, description to route, schema to shape the arguments.

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Hold those three, and everything else follows.

14
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Here's the idea that reframes the whole lesson.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Claude decides when to call a tool based on two things, the user's request and the tool's description.

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:18,000
That description is, by far, the single most important factor in how well your tools perform.

17
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Read that again, because developers underweight it constantly.

18
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Claude reads the description to judge whether this particular tool fits the request in front of it.

19
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
If the prose is thin, the model is left guessing at your intent, and it guesses wrong.

20
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:40,000
A terse label, like handler or process, tells Claude nothing about when the tool applies.

21
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Precise prose is what turns a vague request into the right, well-formed call.

22
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
The description is the router.

23
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Let's separate two things that get conflated, the name and the description.

24
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
The name is an identifier. Your code uses it to invoke the tool.

25
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
And the API uses it to match the returned call back to your function.

26
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
That's real work, but it's execution work, not routing work.

27
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:11,000
The description is different. It's the natural language signal Claude actually reasons over when

28
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:12,000
it's choosing among several tools.

29
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
So, when Claude picks the wrong tool, your instinct might be to rename it.

30
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
But that rarely helps.

31
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Improving the description is almost always the first and best fix.

32
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Name for execution, description for routing.

33
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:32,000
When selection goes wrong, reach for the description first, not the name.

34
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,000
So what belongs in a strong description? Four things.

35
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
First, what the tool does.

36
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
State the capability in plain language so Claude knows what it's for.

37
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Second, when to use it.

38
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Name the kinds of requests and situations where this tool is the right choice.

39
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Third, and people skip this one, when not to use it.

40
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:02,000
If another tool fits a nearby case better, say so, because that prevents misroutes between similar tools.

41
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Fourth, caveats and limits.

42
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Explain what each parameter means, any side effects, and the boundaries the model should respect.

43
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,000
What it does, when to use it, when not to, and the caveats.

44
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Cover those four, and Claude has what it needs to choose well.

45
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
You might think a good schema could carry the load by itself.

46
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
It can't, and it's worth understanding why.

47
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,000
A JSON schema tells Claude what's structurally valid.

48
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:35,000
It says this field is a string, this one's required, this number has to be an integer.

49
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,000
What it can't express is usage patterns.

50
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:45,000
It can't tell Claude when a call actually makes sense, or which optional fields to include in which situation.

51
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,000
That kind of when and why only lives in the description.

52
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,000
So think of it as a division of labor.

53
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:58,000
The schema constrains the shape of the arguments, and the description drives the decision to call in the first place.

54
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
You need both, doing different jobs.

55
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Here's a practical way to think about writing the description.

56
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Treat it like onboarding notes, for a new hire who has never seen this tool before.

57
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
They need context, not a label.

58
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:17,000
Aim for at least three to four sentences, and more if the tool is complex.

59
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Explain what each parameter means, and how it shapes the tool's behavior.

60
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Spell out the edge cases, instead of assuming Claude will infer them from the name.

61
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,000
The reflex is to write a clever, compact name, and a one-line description.

62
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Resist it. Detailed prose beats a clever name every single time, because the name is nearly invisible to rooting.

63
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
And the prose is everything.

64
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Let me make this concrete with a contrast between a vague description and a precise one.

65
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Picture a tool named GetData, whose entire description is just the words DataTool.

66
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Claude has no idea what data, from where, or when, this beats some other tool.

67
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
So it either skips it, or calls it blindly.

68
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
Now picture a tool named SearchOrders, described as retrieving a customer's orders, for support

69
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
questions about past purchases.

70
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
That version roots cleanly.

71
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Claude knows exactly when it applies, and what to put in the arguments.

72
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Same underlying function, wildly different behavior.

73
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
The difference isn't the code behind the tool.

74
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,000
It's entirely in how clearly you described what the tool is for, and when to reach for it.

75
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
So what do you do when Claude keeps ignoring a tool, or calling it at the wrong time?

76
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Go to the description first.

77
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,000
A skipped tool almost always has a thin, underspecified description.

78
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Add the intent, spell out the conditions where it applies, and give a concrete example or two of the

79
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
kind of request that should trigger it.

80
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Do that before you start adjusting temperature, or reaching for a bigger model, because those rarely

81
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:55,000
fix a routing problem.

82
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:03,000
In practice, sharper description prose recovers more bad tool choices than any other single change you can make.

83
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:08,000
When selection misbehaves, rewrite the words before you touch anything else.

84
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:13,000
A tool definition is name, description, and input schema.

85
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,000
And the description carries the routing.

86
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:20,000
It matters because Claude selects tools from that prose, not the name or the schema.

87
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:27,000
Keep in mind, a vague description is the top cause for misrouting, so write real sentences of intent.

88
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,000
That covers tool descriptions as the routing contract.

89
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
I'll see you in the next video.

